{"patch":{"config":{"title":"Antranig Basman","baseURL":"https://ponder.org.uk","defaultContentLanguage":"en","mainSections":["post","posts"],"author":{"name":"Antranig Basman","website":"/","email":"amb26web@ponder.org.uk","twitter":"amb26ponder"},"params":{"homeTitle":"Antranig Basman","subtitle":"Building habitable technologies","bio":"img/20230209_135356 - bio - cropped.jpg","logo":"img/pond.jpg","favicon":"img/favicon.ico","dateFormat":"January 2, 2006","comments":true,"socialShare":true,"showRelatedPosts":true,"commentsLayer":"beautifulWend.comments.giscus","customCSS":["css/ponder.css"],"mediaIcons":[{"title":"Twitter profile","href":"http://twitter.com/amb26ponder","icon":"fab fa-twitter"},{"title":"Send me a message","href":"mailto:amb26webpage@ponder.org.uk","icon":"fa fa-envelope"},{"title":"Antranig's GitHub profile","href":"http://github.com/amb26","icon":"fab fa-github-alt"},{"title":"My papers on DBLP","href":"https://dblp.org/pid/68/5013.html","icon":"fa fa-university"},{"title":"My LinkedIn profile","href":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/antranigbasman/","icon":"fab fa-linkedin"}],"giscus":{"repo":"amb26/ponder","repoId":"R_kgDOOKllGA","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOOKllGM4CvhAS"}},"menu":{"main":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5},{"name":"wend","parent":"docs","url":"docs/wend","weight":6},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5}]}},"features":{"selfHosted":true,"hljs":true,"katex":false}},"pages":[{"kind":"page","type":"post","section":"post","title":"On Development Speed","date":"2025-03-14T00:00:00.000Z","relPermalink":"/post/2025-03-14-development-speed/","permalink":"https://ponder.org.uk/post/2025-03-14-development-speed/","outputPath":"/post/2025-03-14-development-speed/index.html","params":{"title":"On Development Speed","date":"2025-03-14"},"sourcePath":"content/post/2025-03-14-development-speed.md","content":"<p>A recent posting\n<a href=\"https://dev.to/holasoymalva/why-the-latest-javascript-frameworks-are-a-waste-of-time-52pc\">Why the Latest JavaScript Frameworks Are a Waste of Time</a>\nsparks a short train of thought. As well as the characteristically disrespectful tone of discussion that technical\npeople often adopt, and the suggestion, contrary to my speculations looking at\n<a href=\"/post/2025-02-07-benchmarking-dom-reconciliation\">DOM reconciliation</a>, that people really do use low-level benchmarks\nwhen choosing frameworks, is the notion of frameworks contributing to development speed. One commentor invites us to watch\nhim against the clock to implement a front end in Svelte rather than React.</p>\n<p>Whilst I don’t doubt the reality that some frameworks, to some people, are likely to be much more productive\nthan others, this discussion to me ignores a reality is only heightened by the intrusion of AI into coding.\n<a href=\"https://colinclark.org/\">Colin Clark</a>\nremarked to me last week, during his exciting residency at <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob%27s_Pillow\">Jacob’s Pillow</a>\nabout his and his colleagues’ experiences of coding with AI,</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“You go extremely fast, then suddenly you are completely unable to go at all”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Short-run coding speed in essentially any popular framework is shortly, if not already, essentially infinite since\nan AI co-pilot will generate substantial implementations on the spot. A more pertinent question is what risks are being\nstored up as designs become larger and older – these are necessarily hard to quantify since they refer to “<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_risk\">tail risks</a>”\nthat may not come to fruition over the lifetime of a project. But the question is – how likely is it that a design\nis steered to a point where it becomes essentially unnavigable as a result of inability to connect design changes\npredictably with consequences?</p>\n<!-- Find a link about how AI generated code is hard to maintain, and also AI is bad at maintenance -->\n<p>There are a few kinds of responses to such tail risks. One is to avoid frameworks and focus on relatively transparent\nenvironments such as C, as recommended by\n<a href=\"http://www.codersnotes.com/notes/learning-via-bullshit/\">Richard Mitton commenting on a Vyacheslav Egorov talk</a>. In these\nenvironments there is a relatively direct connection between expressions (source code) and results (in this case\nrepresented by emitted assembler). I am interested to see if these extremes can be harmonised – whether there\ncould be kinds of frameworks or <a href=\"/term/substrate\">substrates</a> which allow for a more direct correspondence between\nexpressions and results in the space of a user interface – in particular, which allow the full train of design\ndecisions leading to a particular interface element being a certain way to be traced back to their original\nexpressions from the interface itself. We have a precedent for such systems inside the powerful CSS inspectors\nnow built into every mainstream browser.</p>\n<p>Such a system will be helpful for human and AI coders alike – since easy access to the textual form of the\nrelevant design expression in a system gives a purchase point to an AI copilot just as readily as it does to a human\ntypist. This guards against the risk that systems become unnavigable as a result of the <em>context</em> of a design\nelement becoming unmanageably large. An AI coder is limited by the context required to understand a UI behaviour,\nand opacity linking effects with causes, in the same way as a human.</p>\n","summary":"A recent posting Why the Latest JavaScript Frameworks Are a Waste of Time sparks a short train of thought. As well as the characteristically disrespectful tone of discussion that technical people often adopt, and the suggestion, contrary to my speculations looking at DOM reconciliation , that people really do use low-level benchmarks when choosing frameworks, is the notion of frameworks contributing to development speed. One commentor invites us to watch him against the clock to implement a front end in Svelte rather than React.","summaryText":"A recent posting Why the Latest JavaScript Frameworks Are a Waste of Time sparks a short train of thought. As well as the characteristically disrespectful tone of discussion that technical people often adopt, and the suggestion, contrary to my speculations looking at DOM reconciliation , that people really do use low-level benchmarks when choosing frameworks, is the notion of frameworks contributing to development speed. One commentor invites us to watch him against the clock to implement a front end in Svelte rather than React.","truncated":true,"prevInSection":{"title":"Benchmarking DOM reconciliation algorithms","relPermalink":"/post/2025-02-07-benchmarking-dom-reconciliation/"},"nextInSection":{"title":"On UI as code","relPermalink":"/post/2025-03-20-ui-as-code/"}}],"shared":{"lang":"en","menu":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3,"children":[{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs